decisions:
  - id: D-grid-over-tiling
    decision: Replace TilingEngine binary-tree with explicit NxM GridPreset for ContainerView
    rationale: User wants predictable grid layouts (1x1, 2x2, 3x2) they choose upfront, not dynamic binary splits. WorkspaceGrid already computed fixed cells — just needed explicit cols/rows instead of auto-calculation from instance count.
  - id: D-sidebar-tabs
    decision: Sidebar uses 4 tabs (Sessions/Monitor/Sentinel/Settings) instead of single scroll
    rationale: Sidebar content is too diverse for one scroll view. Tabs let the user focus on what they need. Sessions is the primary tab, Monitor shows groups + tasks + health, Sentinel shows live events, Settings manages workspace preferences.
  - id: D-help-sections
    decision: 9 help sections with sidebar navigation and split-pane layout
    rationale: Covers all major features including experimental ones (voice, gaze). 'What's Rough' section is honest about known limitations — sets expectations and builds trust.
learnings:
  - ContainerView TilingEngine was overengineered for the actual use case — fixed grid is simpler and more predictable
  - ControlPanelTab enum was defined inside ContainerView — moving it to its own location in ControlPanelContainer.swift fixed cross-file reference issues
  - SwiftUI sheet presentation from toolbar buttons works cleanly for help overlay without disrupting the main layout
